network modules, specifically
 attention and sparse neural networks, operate under limited-range constraints.
 To address this constraint, we develop a novel unit called RangeGate,
 which acts as a learned soft clamp that adapts to the specific dynamics
 of attention and sparse networks. Through dynamic adjustment of RangeGate's
 parameters, we optimize the unit's limited-range output to either maximize
 the network's classification performance or facilitate easy inversion of the
 output, depending on whether the network is used for classification or
 inference tasks. In experiments, RangeGate enhances the performance of
 non-contrastive unsupervised learning, exemplies efficient self-supervised
 learning through contrastive memory inference, and yields improved results
 in a variety of sparse and attention-based models.  The code for RangeGate
